@page "/contribute"

<PageTitle>Contribute a lesson · Scriban Tutorial</PageTitle>

<article class="reference">
    <header class="reference__header">
        <div class="reference__eyebrow">Contribute</div>
        <h1>Contribute a lesson</h1>
    </header>
    <section class="reference__intro">
        <p>
            Everything you read in a lesson — the explanations, the
            exercises, the expected outputs — is a plain text file in
            the project's GitHub repository. You don't need to be a
            programmer to edit one. You don't need to install anything.
            You don't even need to leave the browser.
        </p>
        <h2 class="reference__intro-h">Where the content lives</h2>
        <p>
            The repository is at
            <a href="https://github.com/sergeiosipov/scriban-tutorial"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/sergeiosipov/scriban-tutorial</a>.
            The course content lives in this folder:
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/sergeiosipov/scriban-tutorial/tree/main/src/ScribanTutorial/wwwroot/lessons"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>src/ScribanTutorial/wwwroot/lessons/</code></a>
        </p>
        <p>
            Inside, every lesson is a numbered folder (e.g.
            <code>01-blocks/</code>, <code>04-variables/</code>).
            Inside each lesson folder you'll find a
            <code>01-theory.md</code> file (the prose at the top of
            the page) and an <code>02-exercises/</code> folder
            containing one sub-folder per exercise. Each exercise is
            five small files: a description, a JSON data model, the
            expected output, a starter template, and a known-good
            solution. The "File layout" section below has a diagram.
        </p>
        <h2 class="reference__intro-h">How to edit a file (no install required)</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>
                Sign in to GitHub (free account; create one if you don't
                have it). Then open the file you want to change on
                github.com — for example,
                <a href="https://github.com/sergeiosipov/scriban-tutorial/blob/main/src/ScribanTutorial/wwwroot/lessons/01-blocks/01-theory.md"
                   target="_blank" rel="noopener">the theory file for lesson 01</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                Click the pencil icon (<strong>✎ Edit this file</strong>)
                at the top-right of the file view. GitHub will tell you
                "You need to fork this repository to propose changes."
                Click <strong>Fork this repository</strong> — it's a
                one-click copy of the project into your own account.
            </li>
            <li>
                Edit the text in the box. Markdown is what you see in
                the lesson — headings start with <code>#</code>, lists
                start with <code>-</code>, code blocks are fenced with
                triple backticks. The "Theory markdown conventions"
                section below has the full cheat-sheet.
            </li>
            <li>
                Scroll down and click <strong>Commit changes…</strong>.
                A small dialog asks for a short description ("Fix typo
                in lesson 03" is fine) and which branch to commit to —
                accept the default (<em>"Create a new branch…"</em>).
            </li>
            <li>
                Click <strong>Propose changes</strong>, then
                <strong>Create pull request</strong>. That's it — you've
                submitted your edit.
            </li>
        </ol>
        <h2 class="reference__intro-h">What happens next</h2>
        <p>
            A pull request (PR) is a request to merge your edits into the
            live project. As soon as you open one, GitHub Actions runs
            the build and the test suite automatically; you'll see a
            green check (or a red cross) within a couple of minutes
            right on the PR page. The maintainer reviews the PR, may
            ask for small changes (just click <strong>✎ Edit</strong> on
            the file in your branch to update it), then merges it. The
            merge auto-deploys to the live site within ~5 minutes.
        </p>
        <h2 class="reference__intro-h">If you also want to run it locally</h2>
        <p>
            Not required — the GitHub web flow above is enough for prose
            and exercise edits. But if you'd like to preview your
            changes before submitting, install the .NET 10 SDK, clone
            the repository, and run <code>dotnet run</code> from the
            <code>src/ScribanTutorial</code> directory. The README has
            the full setup. Local <code>dotnet test</code> runs the
            same checks CI does on your PR, so you can iterate without
            round-tripping through GitHub.
        </p>
        <p class="reference__intro-foot">
            The full authoring reference is in the next section — more
            detail than most lesson edits need, but it's where the
            file-format rules, the verification commands, and the
            common pitfalls all live.
        </p>
        <p class="reference__intro-foot">
            Working on the app itself — C#, JS, build, CI, or
            infrastructure — rather than lesson content? See
            <a href="https://github.com/sergeiosipov/scriban-tutorial/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md"
               target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code></a>
            for prerequisites, the test suite, the build pipeline,
            and the project-mechanics notes (CodeMirror vendoring,
            GitHub Pages CDN cache).
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="reference__section" id="authoring">
        <div class="reference__body">
            <h2>Authoring reference</h2>

            <h3>File layout</h3>
<pre><code>src/ScribanTutorial/wwwroot/
  manifest.json
  lessons/
    04-variables/
      01-theory.md                 ← the read-aloud part of the lesson
      02-exercises/
        hello/
          01-description.md        ← what the exercise asks
          02-datamodel.json        ← JSON the template will see
          03-expected.txt          ← byte-exact expected output
          04-template.txt          ← starter (with ??? placeholders)
          05-solution.txt          ← known-good solution
          06-cases.json            ← optional hidden inputs (see below)
        local-variable/
          ...
    05-objects/
      ...</code></pre>
            <p>Numeric prefixes are part of the filename. They:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>keep a directory listing in the right order,</li>
                <li>make the first build-step's output match the source order, and</li>
                <li>let the manifest reference exercise dirs by their stable slug (<code>hello</code>, not <code>01-hello</code>).</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Don't rename them.</p>
            <p>
                ContentBuilder also writes generated siblings into each
                lesson and exercise directory at build time: <code>*.html</code>
                for every <code>.md</code>, <code>02-datamodel.html</code>,
                and a <code>bundle.json</code> collecting all the runtime
                inputs in one fetch (six, plus the derived hidden checks when
                <code>06-cases.json</code> is present). These are gitignored —
                don't commit them.
            </p>

            <h3>Add a new exercise (quick)</h3>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Pick a lesson folder under
                    <code>src/ScribanTutorial/wwwroot/lessons/</code>,
                    e.g. <code>06-arrays/</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Inside its <code>02-exercises/</code>, create a new
                    directory named after the exercise's stable slug
                    (kebab-case, ASCII):
                    <code>06-arrays/02-exercises/array-slice/</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Create <strong>all five required files</strong>
                    (encoding rules below — get this part right or the
                    runner will reject your output), plus an optional
                    sixth if the exercise is easy to game by hardcoding
                    the expected output:
                    <table>
                        <thead>
                            <tr><th>File</th><th>What goes in it</th></tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td><code>01-description.md</code></td><td>What the exercise asks. Markdown. <strong>Do not paste the expected output here</strong> — that lives in <code>03-expected.txt</code> and renders in the Expected output panel.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>02-datamodel.json</code></td><td>JSON object the engine binds to. Top-level MUST be an object, not an array.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>03-expected.txt</code></td><td>Byte-exact expected output. CRLF collapsed to LF and trailing newlines trimmed before comparison; everything else significant.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>04-template.txt</code></td><td>Starter template. Use <code>???</code> for the placeholder(s) the learner fills in.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>05-solution.txt</code></td><td>The canonical, known-good solution. Verified automatically by the test suite.</td></tr>
                            <tr><td><code>06-cases.json</code> <em>(optional)</em></td><td>JSON array of alternative data models, each the same shape as the <code>02-datamodel.json</code> root. At build time ContentBuilder renders <code>05-solution.txt</code> against every case and records the outputs as hidden checks — a submission must pass all of them, not just the visible expected output. <code>--verify</code> validates the array's shape and that every case renders.</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </li>
                <li>
                    Open <code>wwwroot/manifest.json</code> and add an entry
                    to the lesson's <code>exercises</code> array (order
                    matters — learners see them in this order):
<pre><code>{ "id": "array-slice", "path": "lessons/06-arrays/02-exercises/array-slice" }</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>
                    From the repo root, <strong>verify</strong> your solution
                    produces the expected output:
<pre><code>dotnet run --project tools\ContentBuilder -- --verify src\ScribanTutorial\wwwroot\lessons\06-arrays\02-exercises\array-slice</code></pre>
                    Expected: <code>--verify OK (...)</code>. On
                    <code>--verify FAIL</code> the tool prints expected vs.
                    actual — fix the solution or the expected file until
                    they agree.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Re-run <strong>the full test suite</strong> to catch any
                    regressions:
<pre><code>dotnet test</code></pre>
                    The <code>ExerciseSolutionTests</code> class
                    auto-includes your new exercise — no test code to write.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>dotnet build</code> (regenerates the
                    <code>.html</code> siblings) → reload the dev server
                    → click the lesson → confirm your new exercise appears
                    and the solution passes when typed in.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Commit. The same path verifies in CI on push, gating
                    the deploy.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <h3>Add a new lesson (start to finish)</h3>
            <p>
                Higher friction than a single exercise — touches the
                manifest in more places and needs a theory file.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    Create the lesson directory (slot it numerically into
                    the course order — <code>11-…</code> puts it after Best
                    practices; <code>08a-…</code> would sit between
                    Expressions and Statements):
<pre><code>src/ScribanTutorial/wwwroot/lessons/11-advanced/</code></pre>
                </li>
                <li>
                    Inside it, create <code>01-theory.md</code> and an empty
                    <code>02-exercises/</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Write the theory. At minimum: one or two
                    <code>## Section</code> headings and one
                    <code>:::example</code> block per concept.
                    <strong>Do not</strong> start the file with a
                    <code># Title</code> — the lesson page already renders
                    the title from the manifest.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Add at least one exercise inside <code>02-exercises/</code>
                    — follow the per-exercise quick start above.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Add the lesson to <code>manifest.json</code>'s
                    <code>lessons</code> array, in the order learners should
                    see it:
<pre><code>{
  "id": "11-advanced",
  "title": "Advanced",
  "theoryPath": "lessons/11-advanced/01-theory",
  "exercises": [
    { "id": "first", "path": "lessons/11-advanced/02-exercises/first" }
  ]
}</code></pre>
                    <code>id</code> must match the directory name.
                    <code>theoryPath</code> omits the extension — the
                    runtime fetches <code>{theoryPath}.html</code> which
                    ContentBuilder writes from your <code>.md</code>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <code>dotnet build</code> → <code>dotnet test</code> →
                    reload.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Spot-check the new lesson in both light and dark themes —
                    code blocks should be readable in both.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <h3>File format rules (critical)</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Rule</th><th>Why</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>UTF-8 with <strong>no BOM</strong></td><td>The runner compares bytes; a BOM prefix breaks byte-exact match against expected.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>LF line endings (<code>\n</code>), not CRLF</td><td><code>.gitattributes</code> already enforces this on commit, but configure your editor too.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>JSON parses cleanly with quoted keys, no trailing commas</td><td>The data model is <code>System.Text.Json</code>-parsed at build time and again at runtime.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>03-expected.txt</code> is byte-exact (except CRLF and trailing newlines)</td><td>Interior whitespace, tabs, and trailing spaces <em>inside a line</em> all count.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>02-datamodel.json</code> top-level is an object</td><td>The Scriban global scope is keyed on object property names; an array at top-level has nothing to bind.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Don't rename exercise / lesson <code>id</code> after release</td><td>Learners' saved progress is keyed <code>{lessonId}:{exerciseId}</code> in localStorage; a rename silently looks like a brand-new (unstarted) exercise.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3>Theory markdown conventions</h3>
            <h4>Headings</h4>
            <p>
                <strong>Do not start the file with a <code># Title</code>
                heading.</strong> The lesson page already renders the title
                from the manifest (<code>lessons[].title</code>) above the
                theory body — a leading <code># Title</code> in the markdown
                produces a duplicated title on screen. Open the file with
                the first paragraph of prose.
            </p>
            <p>
                Use <code>## Section</code> for the main beats and
                <code>### Subsection</code> sparingly.
            </p>
            <h4>Plain code blocks</h4>
            <p>
                Fenced blocks with a language hint get syntax highlighted
                at build time. Supported language hints:
                <code>scriban</code> (highlights via the custom grammar),
                <code>json</code>, <code>text</code> (no highlighting —
                verbatim with HTML escaping). Other language hints fall
                through to TextMateSharp's bundled grammars if present.
            </p>
            <h4>Inline HTML is sanitised</h4>
            <p>
                Markdig passes raw HTML in your <code>.md</code> through to
                the output, but ContentBuilder runs the result through an
                HTML sanitizer before writing the <code>.html</code>. That
                strips <code>&lt;script&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>,
                <code>&lt;object&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;embed&gt;</code>,
                <code>on*=</code> handlers, and <code>javascript:</code> URLs.
                The <code>&lt;span class="hl-*"&gt;</code> highlight wrappers
                and the <code>&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class="language-*"&gt;</code>
                blocks the renderer emits are explicitly allowed.
            </p>
            <h4><code>:::example</code> side-by-side panels</h4>
            <p>
                For "given this template + this data, you get this output"
                — the most common shape a lesson needs:
            </p>
<pre><code>:::example
```scriban
{{ user.first_name }} {{ user.last_name }}
```
```json
{ "user": { "first_name": "Ada", "last_name": "Lovelace" } }
```
```text
Ada Lovelace
```
:::</code></pre>
            <p>
                One <code>scriban</code> block becomes the Template column,
                one <code>json</code> block (optional) becomes the Data
                column, one <code>text</code> block becomes the Output
                column.
            </p>

            <h3>Exercise design</h3>
            <p>A few hard-earned rules:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>One concept per exercise.</strong> Hello (just <code>{{ name }}</code>) and Member access (<code>{{ user.first_name }}</code>) split rather than combining them.</li>
                <li><strong>Starter template is close enough to correct that the gap is obvious.</strong> Use <code>???</code> for the blank.</li>
                <li><strong>Expected output is short and visually distinctive.</strong> A 20-line expected hides off-by-one whitespace bugs from the diff view.</li>
                <li><strong>Always include <code>05-solution.txt</code>.</strong> Run <code>--verify</code> and the test suite before committing.</li>
                <li><strong>No host-config dependencies.</strong> Custom renamers, <code>include "..."</code>, host-provided functions — none of those transfer to a learner's future projects.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Verifying your work</h3>
            <p><strong>Single exercise:</strong></p>
<pre><code>dotnet run --project tools\ContentBuilder -- --verify src\ScribanTutorial\wwwroot\lessons\&lt;lesson&gt;\02-exercises\&lt;exercise&gt;</code></pre>
            <p>
                Exit 0 + <code>--verify OK</code> = green. Exit 1 + diff =
                something's off; the tool prints the expected and actual
                blocks so you can see exactly where.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Everything at once:</strong></p>
<pre><code>dotnet test</code></pre>
            <p>
                The <code>ExerciseSolutionTests</code> xUnit class reads
                the manifest, walks every exercise, and asserts the
                solution renders the expected output. New exercises are
                picked up automatically.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Both at once during a build:</strong></p>
<pre><code>dotnet build</code></pre>
            <p>
                The MSBuild <code>BuildContent</code> target invokes
                ContentBuilder to regenerate the <code>.html</code>
                siblings on every build.
            </p>

            <h3>Editing on Windows / VS Code</h3>
            <p>Settings that prevent the format rules from biting you:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "files.encoding": "utf8",
  "files.eol": "\n",
  "files.insertFinalNewline": false,
  "files.trimTrailingWhitespace": false
}</code></pre>
            <p>
                <code>insertFinalNewline: false</code> because some
                exercises don't want one.
                <code>trimTrailingWhitespace: false</code> because
                <code>03-expected.txt</code> can legitimately contain
                trailing spaces inside a line.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you use PowerShell to write a file by hand,
                <strong>never</strong> use <code>&gt;</code> or default
                <code>Out-File</code>: it writes UTF-16 with a BOM. The
                right pattern:
            </p>
<pre><code>Set-Content -Encoding utf8NoBOM -Path $path -Value $content</code></pre>

            <h3>Manifest field reference</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>courseTitle</code></td><td>string</td><td>Top of the sidebar</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>courseSubtitle</code></td><td>string</td><td>Below the title</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>lessons[]</code></td><td>array</td><td>Order matters — sidebar order</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>lessons[].id</code></td><td>string</td><td>URL slug — must match the folder name</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>lessons[].title</code></td><td>string</td><td>Display name</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>lessons[].theoryPath</code></td><td>string</td><td>Path without extension; runtime fetches <code>.html</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>lessons[].exercises[]</code></td><td>array</td><td>Order matters — exercise order within the lesson</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>lessons[].exercises[].id</code></td><td>string</td><td>Stable identifier — <strong>don't rename</strong> after release</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>lessons[].exercises[].path</code></td><td>string</td><td>Path to the exercise directory (no trailing slash)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>

            <h3>Common authoring pitfalls</h3>
            <table>
                <thead>
                    <tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>--verify FAIL</code> with no obvious diff</td><td>Trailing whitespace <strong>inside</strong> a line of <code>03-expected.txt</code> you didn't notice</td><td>Toggle "render whitespace" in your editor and re-check</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Exercise renders nothing on the page</td><td>Template renders the empty string because the data-model property name is misspelled (Scriban returns null silently)</td><td>Double-check <code>02-datamodel.json</code> field names against the template</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>for x in items</code> outputs nothing</td><td><code>items</code> is missing from the data model, or it's null</td><td>Confirm the field exists and is an array</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Extra blank line in output</td><td>Block tags like <code>{{ for }}</code> or <code>{{ end }}</code> leave the surrounding newlines in</td><td>Add <code>{{-</code> / <code>-}}</code> whitespace control. See lessons 01 (Blocks) and 09 (Statements).</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Browser still shows old content</td><td>GitHub Pages CDN cache — 2–5 min after a deploy lands</td><td>Hard refresh (Ctrl+F5), wait</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Sidebar indicator stays ○ even after passing</td><td>Browser blocked <code>localStorage</code> (private mode, restrictive content-blocker)</td><td>Try a normal window</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>
